You are building the first screen of a small internal support desk. A dispatcher opens it, sees the tickets waiting on them, narrows the list to the ones that need attention now, and raises a new ticket while a customer is still on the phone.

The data layer is already decided. `src/api/types.ts` defines a `TicketApi` interface — `listTickets`, `getTicket`, `createTicket`, `listComments` — and `src/api/mockApi.ts` implements it in memory. The mock behaves like a network rather than like a local array: it is asynchronous, it can be told to be slow, it can be told to fail, and it validates on its own side, so your form check is fast feedback and never the last word.

That interface is the whole point of the mission. In M02 you replace the mock with an adapter that speaks HTTP to a real backend, and `src/main.tsx` is the only file that should have to change. If a component imports the mock directly, or assumes tickets already exist in an array, the swap stops being a one-line change and becomes a rewrite.

Four features are missing, and `src/App.tsx` currently renders a heading and a sentence saying so:

1. a ticket list showing title, status and customer;
2. a status filter across all, open, pending and closed;
3. a create form with validation the user can actually perceive;
4. a ticket detail view reachable at its own `/tickets/:id` route.

The hard part is not any one of those. It is that four states — loading, empty, error and loaded — have to stay distinguishable from one another, that a slow response must not leave a stuck spinner or apply a result the user has already moved past, and that a rejected field has to announce itself to a screen reader rather than only turning red.

Every customer, ticket and comment in this repository is fictional. There are no credentials, no external services and nothing to configure beyond `npm install`.
